h the otherwise injection-heavy pattern for LHRH antagonists. This makes it a notable logistical alternative within the ADT drug-class cluster. [Data: Entities (3531, 3532, 3529); Relationships (7195, 7187)]\\n\\nThe data also provides a direct brand association: Orgovyx is named as the brand name for relugolix. Such naming links clinical drug identity to a commercially recognizable label within the network. [Data: Entities (3531, 3539); Relationships (7196)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Anti-androgens and pill-based regimens introduce additional ADT-related options and distinct adherence considerations\",\n      \"explanation\": \"Anti-androgens appear as a named drug class within the hormone therapy/ADT context, with nilutamide, flutamide, and bicalutamide explicitly listed as options. This expands the ADT sub-network beyond LHRH agonists/antagonists into agents that block androgen action. [Data: Entities (3530, 3540, 3541, 3542); Relationships (7190, 7191, 7192)]\\n\\nSeparately, the community includes a general \\\"pills\\\" entity describing oral medication use patterns (e.g., taken one to three times per day depending on the medication) and notes that pills can include drug classes such as anti-androgens and androgen synthesis inhibitors. This connects certain ADT-adjacent options to oral adherence and dosing-frequency considerations, contrasting with injection schedule logistics. [Data: Entities (3563, 3570); Relationships (7234)]\"\n    },\n    {\n      \"summary\": \"Medication-based testosterone lowering is described as reversible, whereas orchiectomy is permanent and equated with surgical castration\",\n      \"explanation\": \"The community distinguishes between drug-based hormone-lowering medication and surgical removal of the testicles. Hormone-lowering medication is described as a reversible method when stopped, and this reversibility is explicitly tied to medication stoppage. [Data: Entities (3525, 3564); Relationships (7181)]\\n\\nIn contr